ROWS = [
dict(id="T135", d="RW", t="Singularity of harmonic measure for random walks on cocompact Fuchsian groups (Kaimanovich–Le Prince)",
 s="Let Γ be a cocompact lattice in PSL_2(R), for example the fundamental group of a closed hyperbolic surface, and μ a finitely supported probability measure whose support generates Γ as a semigroup. The harmonic (hitting) measure on the boundary circle of the random walk with step law μ is singular with respect to Lebesgue measure.",
 c="Kaimanovich and Le Prince conjectured singularity for every finitely supported random walk on a discrete subgroup of a semisimple Lie group; the cocompact Fuchsian case is the test case, because there the boundary circle carries both measures naturally.",
 src="V. A. Kaimanovich and V. Le Prince, Matrix random products with singular harmonic measure, Geom. Dedicata 150 (2011)",
 why="It asks whether the stationary measure of a random walk, which can be made smooth by spreading the steps out (Furstenberg), can be absolutely continuous when the steps are finitely supported; the answer ties the entropy, drift and growth of a random walk to the geometry of the group.",
 st="Kaimanovich and Le Prince showed that every such group carries finitely supported walks with singular harmonic measure. Singularity is proved for every finitely supported walk on non-cocompact lattices (Guivarc'h–Le Jan, Ann. Sci. ÉNS 26 (1993); Gadre–Maher–Tiozzo, Comment. Math. Helv. 92 (2017)) and for nearest-neighbour walks with respect to certain generating sets of cocompact Fuchsian groups (Kosenko–Tiozzo, Forum Math. Sigma 10 (2022)). Bourgain (2012) constructed finitely supported measures on SL_2(R), generating non-discrete groups, whose stationary measure is absolutely continuous. Open for general finitely supported walks on cocompact lattices.",
 x="SOLVED (claimed): Kosenko and Tiozzo, Singularity of harmonic measure for finitely supported random walks (arXiv:2609.19115, 16 Sep 2026, unrefereed), resolve the singularity conjecture affirmatively for finitely supported non-degenerate random walks on cocompact Fuchsian groups, which is this row. The proof builds one geodesic current from Lebesgue measure and one from the harmonic measure and shows with Fourier analysis on the boundary that they differ. The Kaimanovich–Le Prince conjecture for discrete subgroups of other semisimple groups is a separate question. Read from the arXiv abstract 14 Oct; flagged by the w673 audit."),
dict(id="T136", d="SM", t="Free-energy and ground-state fluctuations of the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model at low temperature",
 s="For the Ising SK model H_N(σ) = N^{−1/2} Σ_{i<j} g_ij σ_i σ_j with iid standard Gaussian couplings, determine the order and the limiting law of the fluctuations of log Z_N(β) at each fixed β > 1, and of the ground-state energy max_σ H_N(σ). Physics predicts a standard deviation of order N^{1/6}.",
 c="Order N^{1/4} has also been proposed; the survey of Collins-Woodfin and Le records the competing predictions. In the spherical SK model the low-temperature fluctuations are of order N^{1/3} with a Tracy–Widom law (Baik–Lee 2016), which is no guide to the Ising case.",
 src="A. Crisanti, G. Paladin, H.-J. Sommers and A. Vulpiani, Replica trick and fluctuations in disordered systems, J. Phys. I France 2 (1992) 1325–1332; E. Collins-Woodfin and H. T. Le, Free energy fluctuations in SK and related spin glass models: a literature survey, arXiv:2510.26960 (2025), Section 2",
 why="The Parisi formula fixes the limit of the free energy, but nothing fixes the next order; the order of fluctuations is the basic quantitative question about a disordered system beyond its limit, and the ground-state version is the fluctuation question for a random quadratic optimisation problem.",
 st="For β < 1 the fluctuations are of order one and Gaussian (Aizenman–Lebowitz–Ruelle 1987). For β > 1 superconcentration gives variance O(N/log N) (Chatterjee, arXiv:0907.3381, at zero external field). Aronow and Lopatto (arXiv:2609.09103, 2026) prove that at zero external field and every fixed β > 1 the variance of log Z_N(β) lies between the orders N^{4/15} and N^{7/15}, with the same exponents for the ground-state energy; the predicted standard deviation N^{1/6}, a variance of order N^{1/3}, lies inside that range. The competing predictions are surveyed by Collins-Woodfin and Le (arXiv:2510.26960). The exponent and the limit law are open. Partial claim, 14 Nov 2026: the OpenAI math release preprints 'The low-temperature Sherrington-Kirkpatrick free-energy limiting law' and its companion on the fluctuation scale (24 Sep 2026, github.com/openai/math) claim variance of order N^(1/3) and a limiting law for the centred log Z_N(beta) at every fixed beta>1. The ground-state fluctuation exponent and law are not covered, and fixed-beta bounds do not reach them (the error in max H <= log Z / beta is of order N/beta). These are unrefereed AI-written claims; this records their scope, not a check of the proofs.",
 n="Status updated after the w673 audit (14 Oct)."),
dict(id="T137", x="SOLVED (claimed, unrefereed), 14 Nov 2026: D. Panchenko, The Mezard-Parisi formula (arXiv:2610.39545, 29 Oct 2026, Thm 2.1) claims the formula for diluted even-K spin glasses with K >= 2 and for random K-SAT for every K >= 2, with Poisson clauses; the OpenAI math release preprint 'The Mezard-Parisi formula for diluted spin glasses' (23 Sep 2026) claims Viana-Bray, diluted even-K and even-K SAT, and its Lean statement was accepted by a community Comparator run (26 Oct 2026). Unrefereed; this records the claim, not a check of the proof. Replacement row to be screened.", d="SM", t="The Parisi formula for diluted spin glasses",
 s="For the Viana–Bray model, N Ising spins in which each pair interacts, independently with probability c/N, through an independent coupling with the symmetric law ±1 (or the standard Gaussian law), prove that lim (1/N) E log Z_N(β) equals the Mézard–Parisi variational formula at every β > 0, with full replica symmetry breaking where the model needs it. The same question is posed for the diluted K-spin model with K even and for random K-SAT at positive temperature.",
 c="The Mézard–Parisi formula is a hierarchy of distributions over distributions of cavity fields, much heavier than the SK formula. The model, the coupling law and the trial functional have to be fixed; for heavy-tailed couplings the expected free energy need not even be finite. The zero-temperature MaxCut case on sparse random graphs is T122.",
 src="M. Mézard and G. Parisi, The Bethe lattice spin glass revisited, Eur. Phys. J. B 20 (2001); D. Panchenko, Structure of finite-RSB asymptotic Gibbs measures in the diluted spin glass models, arXiv:1406.4702 (2014)",
 why="Sparse random constraint satisfaction problems, MaxCut and colouring of sparse random graphs are diluted spin glasses at zero temperature; the formula would give their ground-state values and would put the cavity method, which predicts their phase transitions, on a rigorous footing.",
 st="The formula is an upper bound for the diluted K-spin model with K even, which includes Viana–Bray, and for random K-SAT (Franz–Leone 2003; Panchenko–Talagrand 2004), and it is proved in replica-symmetric regimes such as high temperature (for Viana–Bray, Guerra–Toninelli, J. Stat. Phys. 115 (2004)). Panchenko showed that 1-RSB asymptotic Gibbs measures of the diluted p-spin models satisfy the Mézard–Parisi ansatz (arXiv:1308.1944), and that after a small modification of the Hamiltonian so do all finite-RSB ones (arXiv:1406.4702); the full-RSB case and the removal of the modification are missing. The satisfiability threshold of random K-SAT at large K is proved to be the one-step value (Ding–Sly–Sun 2015), but that is a zero-temperature statement, not the free energy. The formula at every temperature is open.",
 n="Statement revised after the w673 audit (14 Oct)."),
dict(id="T138", d="RG", t="The algorithmic threshold of random k-SAT",
 s="For random k-SAT with n variables and ⌊αn⌋ independent uniformly random k-clauses, characterise the densities α at which some randomized polynomial-time algorithm finds a satisfying assignment with high probability. Conjecturally the threshold is (1 + o_k(1)) 2^k ln k / k, far below the satisfiability threshold 2^k ln 2 − (1 + ln 2)/2 + o_k(1).",
 c="Hardness for every polynomial-time algorithm cannot be proved without a complexity assumption, so the question as usually posed is to locate the threshold and to prove that broad classes of algorithms (local, low-degree, stable) fail above it. The limits are taken with k fixed and n → ∞, and then k large.",
 src="D. Achlioptas and A. Coja-Oghlan, Algorithmic barriers from phase transitions, FOCS 2008; A. Coja-Oghlan, A better algorithm for random k-SAT, SIAM J. Comput. 39 (2010); D. Gamarnik, The overlap gap property, arXiv:2109.14409 (2021)",
 why="Random k-SAT is the model case of a gap between existence and efficient construction: solutions exist at densities about k/ln k times larger than any known algorithm reaches, and the gap begins where the solution space shatters into clusters.",
 st="Coja-Oghlan's algorithm succeeds up to (1 − ε) 2^k ln k / k, where the solution space shatters (Achlioptas–Coja-Oghlan 2008); the satisfiability threshold for large k is known (Ding–Sly–Sun 2015). Low-degree polynomial algorithms fail above about 4.91 · 2^k ln k / k (Bresler–Huang, FOCS 2021), with stronger low-degree hardness by Huang and Sellke (arXiv:2501.06427), and He, Li and Yang (arXiv:2610.10474) sample solutions almost uniformly for α ≤ 2^k/k^16 at large k. Whether any polynomial-time algorithm succeeds above (1 + ε) 2^k ln k / k is open.",
 n="Status updated after the w673 audit (14 Oct)."),
dict(id="T139", d="COMB", t="Mansour's conjecture on the Fourier spectrum of DNF formulas",
 s="There is a constant C such that for every t ≥ 2 and ε ∈ (0, 1), every Boolean function f on {−1,1}^n computed by a DNF formula with t terms has a polynomial p with at most t^{C log(1/ε)} monomials and E (f − p)² ≤ ε; equivalently, all but ε of the Fourier weight of f lies on that many coefficients.",
 c="With t = 1 the bound would allow a single monomial, which fails for a conjunction of a few literals, hence t ≥ 2. It follows from the Fourier entropy–influence conjecture (T021). Through Gopalan, Kalai and Klivans (2008) it would give a polynomial-time agnostic learning algorithm for DNF formulas under the uniform distribution with membership queries (Gopalan–Kalai–Klivans, Agnostically learning decision trees, STOC 2008); the non-agnostic case is already Jackson's Harmonic Sieve (1997).",
 src="Y. Mansour, An O(n^{log log n}) learning algorithm for DNF under the uniform distribution, J. Comput. System Sci. 50 (1995)",
 why="DNF formulas are the basic class of computational learning theory, and the conjecture is the cleanest statement that their Fourier spectrum is as sparse as their size suggests.",
 st="Mansour proved the bound t^{O(log log t · log(1/ε))}. The conjecture holds for random DNF formulas (Klivans–Lee–Wan, COLT 2010). For DNFs of read number k, Lecomte and Tan (arXiv:2109.04525) prove concentration on t^{O(log log k)} coefficients for constant ε, improving the earlier t^{O(k^{3/2})}, and the optimal poly(t) for k up to about log log t. Open in general.",
 n="Statement revised after the w673 audit (14 Oct)."),
dict(id="T140", d="SM", t="The hard-core model on Z^d in high dimension (Galvin–Kahn)",
 s="For every ε > 0 and all large enough d, the hard-core model on Z^d (the random independent set I weighted by λ^{|I|}) has more than one Gibbs measure, the states favouring the even and the odd sublattice being different, at every fugacity λ ≥ d^{−1+ε}.",
 c="Uniqueness holds for λ below a constant times 1/d, by Dobrushin's condition, and below the uniqueness threshold of the 2d-regular tree by Weitz (STOC 2006), so the conjecture puts the transition at d^{−1+o(1)}. Monotonicity in λ is not known, so the threshold need not be a single number. T109 asks whether there is a single transition on Z^2.",
 src="D. Galvin and J. Kahn, On phase transition in the hard-core model on Z^d, Combin. Probab. Comput. 13 (2004) (exact form of the conjecture to verify)",
 why="The hard-core model is the basic lattice gas with exclusion; its high-dimensional behaviour tests whether lattice phase transitions approach their tree (mean-field) values, and the proofs drove the combinatorics of odd cutsets and containers.",
 st="Phase coexistence for λ ≥ C d^{−1/4} log^{3/4} d (Galvin–Kahn, Combin. Probab. Comput. 13 (2004)) and λ ≥ C d^{−1/3} log² d (Peled–Samotij, Ann. Inst. H. Poincaré Probab. Statist. 50 (2014)), then for λ ≥ C log d/d (Hadas–Peled, arXiv:2603.27144).",
 x="SOLVED (claimed): Hadas and Peled, On the critical fugacity of the hard-core model on regular bipartite graphs (arXiv:2603.27144, 28 Mar 2026), prove long-range order on finite regular bipartite graphs above a threshold set by expansion parameters; on the hypercube and on fixed-side tori this is λ ≥ Ω(log d/d), and reflection positivity carries it to Z^d, so the critical fugacity on Z^d is d^{−1+o(1)}. Read from the arXiv abstract 14 Oct; flagged by the w673 audit."),
dict(id="T141", d="RMT", t="The oriented Kesten–McKay law for random regular digraphs",
 s="For fixed d ≥ 3, the empirical distribution of the eigenvalues of the adjacency matrix of a uniformly random simple d-regular directed graph on n vertices (no loops, in- and out-degree d at every vertex) converges weakly in probability as n → ∞ to the rotation-invariant law on the disc |z| < √d with density d²(d − 1) / (π (d² − |z|²)²), the Brown measure of the free sum of d Haar unitaries.",
 c="Rescaled by √d, the conjectured law tends to the circular law as d → ∞; the conjecture is the fixed-degree counterpart of the Kesten–McKay law for undirected random regular graphs.",
 src="C. Bordenave and D. Chafaï, Around the circular law, Probab. Surv. 9 (2012) 1–89",
 why="It is the basic spectral question for sparse non-Hermitian random matrices; at fixed degree the method behind the circular law, control of the least singular value of the shifted matrix, breaks down.",
 st="The circular law, after rescaling by √d, is proved when d grows at least polylogarithmically in n (Cook 2019; Litvak–Lytova–Tikhomirov–Tomczak-Jaegermann–Youssef 2021) and for sums of d independent uniform permutation matrices with d growing (Basak–Cook–Zeitouni, Electron. J. Probab. 23 (2018)). At fixed d ≥ 3 even the nonsingularity of the adjacency matrix was proved only recently (J. Huang, Duke Math. J. 170 (2021), arXiv:1807.06465). Adhikari and Dembo (arXiv:2310.14132) reduce the conjecture to a bound on the probability that the smallest singular values of the shifted matrices are tiny, and leave that bound open. Coste, Lambert and Zhu (arXiv:2204.00524) find the limit of the characteristic polynomial inside the unit disc for sums of random permutation matrices, which does not give the eigenvalue law. He and Huang (arXiv:2609.05297) prove, for every fixed d ≥ 2, that the empirical eigenvalue distribution converges in probability to the oriented Kesten–McKay law, by a small-ball estimate for the smallest singular value of the shifted matrices and a polynomial hard-edge bound. The preprint is not yet refereed.",
 n="Status updated after the w673 audit (14 Oct). Fame borderline in that audit; kept for now."),
dict(id="T142", d="COMB", t="The variance of the longest common subsequence of two random words (Waterman's conjecture)",
 s="Let L_n be the length of a longest common subsequence of two independent uniformly random binary words of length n. Then Var(L_n) is of order n: there are constants 0 < c < C with c n ≤ Var(L_n) ≤ C n for all large n.",
 c="E L_n / n converges to the Chvátal–Sankoff constant γ_2 ≈ 0.812, whose exact value is unknown; Steele's guess γ_2 = 2/(1 + √2) is ruled out by Lueker's bounds (J. ACM 2009). The upper bound is elementary, so the conjecture is the matching lower bound.",
 src="V. Chvátal and D. Sankoff, Longest common subsequences of two random sequences, J. Appl. Probab. 12 (1975); M. S. Waterman, Estimating statistical significance of sequence alignments, Philos. Trans. R. Soc. Lond. B 344 (1994) 383–390",
 why="The longest common subsequence is the basic score for comparing two sequences (in DNA alignment among others), and its fluctuations decide when an alignment score is significant. It is a directed last-passage percolation problem with dependent weights; independent weights would give variance of order n^{2/3}, so the conjecture says the dependence changes the universality class.",
 st="Steele (1986) proved Var(L_n) ≤ n with the Efron–Stein inequality; Chvátal and Sankoff had conjectured a variance of order o(n^{2/3}), which Waterman's simulations contradict. A linear lower bound is proved when one letter has very small probability (Lember–Matzinger, Ann. Probab. 37 (2009)) and in other strongly biased models (Houdré–Ma, High Dimensional Probability VII, Progr. Probab. 71 (2016)). Houdré and Işlak (arXiv:1408.1559) prove a central limit theorem for L_n assuming a lower bound of the right order on the variance, so the conjecture would also give the fluctuations. Houdré and Liu (arXiv:1812.09552) prove the linear order when one word omits a letter of the alphabet. Amsalu, Hauser and Matzinger (arXiv:1211.5489) show that the linear order follows once gamma(S) - gamma(S - epsilon T) > 0 for the scoring function T induced by changing one letter into another, and verify this by simulation at a stated confidence level for binary words with letter probabilities 0.2 and 0.8; for equiprobable letters the difference vanishes by symmetry, so the criterion says nothing there. Open for uniform binary words.",
 n="Status updated after the w673 audit (14 Oct). Fame borderline in that audit; kept for now."),
dict(id="T143", d="RW", t="The limit law of the cover time of the two-dimensional torus",
 s="Let C_n be the time simple random walk on the discrete torus (Z/nZ)² takes to visit every vertex. There are deterministic a_n such that √(C_n)/n − a_n converges in distribution, conjecturally to a randomly shifted Gumbel law.",
 c="The first-order asymptotics C_n ~ (4/π) n² (log n)² are due to Dembo, Peres, Rosen and Zeitouni; the conjecture is the analogue, for cover times, of the convergence in law of the maximum of the two-dimensional Gaussian free field.",
 src="A. Dembo, Y. Peres, J. Rosen and O. Zeitouni, Cover times for Brownian motion and random walks in two dimensions, Ann. Math. 160 (2004); D. Belius, J. Rosen and O. Zeitouni, Tightness for the cover time of the two dimensional sphere, Probab. Theory Related Fields 176 (2020)",
 why="Two-dimensional cover times are governed by the same log-correlated structure as the maximum of the Gaussian free field and of branching random walk; the limit law is the piece still missing from that picture.",
 st="First order: Dembo–Peres–Rosen–Zeitouni (2004). Second-order terms: Belius–Kistler (Probab. Theory Related Fields 167 (2017)) and Abe (J. Theoret. Probab., 2021). Tightness of the centred square root is proved for Brownian motion on the two-sphere (Belius–Rosen–Zeitouni, Probab. Theory Related Fields 176 (2020)), and convergence to a randomly shifted Gumbel law for the cover time of the binary tree (Cortines–Louidor–Saglietti, Adv. Math. 391 (2021)). Convergence in law on the discrete torus is open (whether tightness is proved there is to verify).",
 x="SOLVED (claimed): Yechi Zhou, A limit law for the cover time of the two-dimensional discrete torus (arXiv:2610.01852, 1 Oct 2026, unrefereed), proves for the walk with jump rate one on (Z/NZ)² that T_N/((2/π) N² log N) − 2 log N + log log N converges in law to G + log(κZ), with G a standard Gumbel variable, Z the total mass of the critical Gaussian multiplicative chaos of the zero-average Gaussian free field on the unit torus, independent of G, and κ > 0 deterministic; the paper transfers this to discrete time. In the row's form, √(C_N)/N − (2/√π) log N + (1/(2√π)) log log N converges to (G + log(κZ))/(2√π). It answers the question of Aldous recorded by Dembo, Peres, Rosen and Zeitouni. Read from the arXiv abstract and the paper's introduction 14 Oct; flagged by the w673 audit."),
dict(id="T144", d="RMT", t="Wigner–Dyson level statistics for the three-dimensional Anderson model at weak disorder",
 s="For the Anderson model H = −Δ + λV on Z^3, with V_x iid with a bounded density (for example uniform on [−1, 1]) and small λ > 0, restricted to the box [−L, L]^3 with periodic boundary conditions, take an energy E in the bulk of the spectrum where the states are extended. The eigenvalues near E, rescaled by the integrated density of states, converge as L → ∞ to the bulk sine-kernel process of the Gaussian orthogonal ensemble (H is real symmetric).",
 c="A genuinely random potential is needed: a constant potential gives the free Laplacian, whose spectrum has degenerate deterministic spacings. In the localized regime the corresponding limit is Poisson. Absolutely continuous spectrum, the subject of T009, would not by itself give GOE statistics.",
 src="B. I. Shklovskii, B. Shapiro, B. R. Sears, P. Lambrianides and H. B. Shore, Statistics of spectra of disordered systems near the metal–insulator transition, Phys. Rev. B 47 (1993) 11487; M. Aizenman and S. Warzel, Random Operators, Grad. Stud. Math. 168 (AMS, 2015)",
 why="Level statistics are the observable signature of the metal–insulator transition and the bridge between random Schrödinger operators and random matrix theory.",
 st="Poisson statistics in the localized regime are proved (Minami 1996; for Hölder single-site laws Dolai–Krishna, arXiv:1408.4251). On the delocalized side GOE statistics are proved for random band matrices of large enough width (T128) but for no Schrödinger operator on Z^d. For uniform potentials at weak disorder, absolutely continuous spectrum near the bottom of the band in every d ≥ 3 is claimed in an unrefereed 2026 preprint (T009, retired); the level statistics are open.",
 n="Statement revised after the w673 audit (14 Oct)."),
dict(id="T145", d="RMT", t="Equality of the microstates and non-microstates free entropies",
 s="For every n-tuple of self-adjoint elements X_1, …, X_n of a tracial von Neumann algebra that embeds in an ultrapower of the hyperfinite II_1 factor (a Connes-embeddable tuple), Voiculescu's microstates free entropy χ(X_1, …, X_n), defined through the volume of the set of n-tuples of matrices whose joint moments approximate those of the X_i, equals his non-microstates free entropy χ*(X_1, …, X_n), defined through free Fisher information.",
 c="Without the embeddability assumption the equality fails: since Connes's embedding problem has a negative answer (Ji–Natarajan–Vidick–Wright–Yuen, MIP* = RE), there are tuples with no microstates, so χ = −∞, and χ* > −∞ (Jekel–Pi, arXiv:2305.02574, introduction). Up to Gaussian normalisation, χ is the exponential rate (a lim sup) at which independent GUE matrices have joint moments near those of the X_i, so equality would identify a large-deviation quantity for random matrices with an entropy defined intrinsically, much as classical entropy is recovered from Fisher information along the heat flow. Whether the corresponding large deviation principle holds is itself open.",
 src="D. Voiculescu, The analogues of entropy and of Fisher's information measure in free probability theory V: noncommutative Hilbert transforms, Invent. Math. 132 (1998) 189–227",
 why="It is the central open problem of free entropy, the theory that connects random matrix large deviations with the structure of operator algebras.",
 st="χ ≤ χ* is proved (Biane–Capitaine–Guionnet 2003, through large deviations for Hermitian Brownian motion), with an elementary proof, extended to conditional free entropies, by Jekel and Pi (arXiv:2305.02574). Equality holds for a single variable and for free families of single variables, since both quantities are additive under freeness (Voiculescu, Invent. Math. 118 (1994) and 132 (1998)). Open for Connes-embeddable tuples.",
 n="Statement revised after the w673 audit (14 Oct)."),
dict(id="T146", d="SM", t="Universality of the critical two-dimensional Ising model",
 s="For every ferromagnetic Ising model on Z^2 with finite-range, translation-invariant pair couplings that are invariant under the symmetries of Z^2 and whose interaction graph is connected, at its critical temperature the spin–spin correlation decays like |x|^{−1/4}, the correlation-length exponent is ν = 1, and the rescaled spin and energy fields converge to the same conformally invariant limits as for the nearest-neighbour model.",
 c="Onsager's exact solution and the fermionic methods behind the scaling limits need a planar nearest-neighbour model; a finite-range model is not planar. Connectivity is needed: couplings only between sites at distance 2 along the axes split Z^2 into four independent Ising models on sublattices.",
 src="Standard renormalisation-group prediction; see H. Duminil-Copin, 100 years of the (critical) Ising model on the hypercubic lattice, Proc. ICM 2022, 164–210",
 why="Universality is the central prediction of the renormalisation group, and the two-dimensional Ising model is the one case where the universal limit is completely known; the question is purely whether non-integrable models reach it.",
 st="Proved for the nearest-neighbour model, also on isoradial graphs (Smirnov 2010; Chelkak–Smirnov 2012; Chelkak–Hongler–Izyurov 2015), and for energy correlations of small finite-range perturbations of it (Giuliani–Greenblatt–Mastropietro, J. Math. Phys. 53 (2012)). For general finite-range models with a connected interaction graph, Aizenman, Duminil-Copin, Tassion and Warzel (arXiv:1801.04960, Theorem 1.2) proved that the boundary spin correlations at criticality are asymptotically Pfaffian; this concerns boundary correlations, not the bulk scaling limits. Open in general.",
 n="Statement revised after the w673 audit (14 Oct)."),
]
